WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.520
Narrator: Listen as Elisabeth Moss

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.167
turns off the sink in this scene.

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:07.924
[water running]

00:00:07.924 --> 00:00:08.757
[handle squeaks]

00:00:08.757 --> 00:00:10.265
[silence]

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.075
Now listen to it with a
completely different sound design.

00:00:15.075 --> 00:00:17.870
[water running]

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:19.503
[handle squeaks]

00:00:19.503 --> 00:00:21.028
[water drains]

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.833
In the first version, all sound cuts out.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.240
But that's a whole lot scarier.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.410
Sound has been an
important element in film

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.390
for a long time.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.765
But in horror movies, it
plays an even bigger role.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.215
Leslie Bloome: When it
comes to horror films,

00:00:40.215 --> 00:00:41.420
you don't want to give away the ghost.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.967
You don't want to give away the monster

00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:44.410
or the moment.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:45.870
Narrator: That's Leslie Bloome,

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.190
cofounder of Alchemy Post Sound.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:50.110
And he just described the key difference

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.760
between regular Foley
sound and what he does

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.690
as a Foley artist for horror movies,

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.400
like "The Invisible Man."

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.266
Normally, Foley focuses on sounds

00:00:58.266 --> 00:00:59.966
made by characters you see.

00:00:59.966 --> 00:01:01.940
But in horror, it's about the sounds

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.756
of the unseen character,

00:01:03.756 --> 00:01:06.356
someone or something that may be there

00:01:06.356 --> 00:01:09.517
without the audience
knowing for sure that it is.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:12.640
That's what ratchets up the suspense

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.290
and primes the audience for
a truly terrifying reveal.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.560
And maybe no film does this better

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.510
than the one with an invisible villain.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.288
Take the simple sound of wind,

00:01:23.288 --> 00:01:25.710
a staple of horror-movie sound design,

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.315
whether it's curtains
reacting to a rush of air...

00:01:28.315 --> 00:01:30.400
[fabric rustles]

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.946
or a flock of birds
flapping against the breeze.

00:01:33.946 --> 00:01:37.260
[gloves slapping]

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.200
In a regular movie, you might hear wind

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.650
when characters open a
window or walk outside.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:43.838
But in horror movies,

00:01:43.838 --> 00:01:46.720
often the source of the
sound won't be clear.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:47.661
For this next part,

00:01:47.661 --> 00:01:50.410
you might want to grab your headphones.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.810
This scene signals the
Invisible Man's presence

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.870
with mysterious whooshing sounds,

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.970
created by flapping an old shirt.

00:01:58.125 --> 00:02:01.440
[fabric rustles]

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.570
You can't actually see where
this wind sound is coming from,

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:07.010
but as Elisabeth Moss' character, Cecilia,

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:10.946
approaches the sink, the
whooshes build in intensity.

00:02:10.946 --> 00:02:13.800
[whooshing]

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.285
And build...

00:02:15.285 --> 00:02:18.560
[whooshing grows louder]

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.236
until each one sounds more like a rumble.

00:02:21.236 --> 00:02:26.236
[water running]
[whooshing intensifies]

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:30.930
Still, the window is closed,

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.750
and the curtains look pretty still.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.860
So the sound suggests something's
moving inside the room.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.360
Exactly where, we don't know.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:39.876
There's another sound in this scene

00:02:39.876 --> 00:02:42.429
that confuses our senses even more.

00:02:42.429 --> 00:02:45.390
[whooshing]
[wind chimes dinging]

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:46.903
Hear those wind chimes?

00:02:48.018 --> 00:02:50.998
[whooshing]
[wind chimes dinging]

00:02:50.998 --> 00:02:53.410
They're subtle, but disorienting.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.440
Especially because the sound isn't coming

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.740
from something you can point
to in the outside world.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.963
See, Leslie uses different
kinds of wind chimes

00:03:00.963 --> 00:03:03.880
to accentuate tense
moments in horror scenes.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.930
[wind chimes dinging]

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.590
Even if there aren't
any chimes in the shot.

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:11.280
It could be that moment that, you know,

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.780
somebody hears a ringing in their ears.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.050
Narrator: That ringing in the ears

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.020
is called a subjective sound --

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.120
something the character's
hearing in their mind.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.880
In horror movies, subjective
sound helps place you

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.750
inside the protagonist's head,

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.230
so you identify with their fear.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.489
Leslie: Then we have the
one that was actually used

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:31.270
in "The Invisible Man."

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:33.390
Narrator: It's how the Foley
artist handles the chimes

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:35.390
that makes the sound creepy.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.422
This is what Leslie calls
a "demented wind chime,"

00:03:38.422 --> 00:03:40.980
[ding echoes]

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.597
a sound Alchemy made
for "The Night House."

00:03:44.563 --> 00:03:46.670
[ringing]

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.983
They created it by putting an
old telephone bell on a rope

00:03:49.983 --> 00:03:52.060
and swinging it around.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.173
The slower you swing it,
the creepier it gets.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:59.740
Even the most common sounds
require special treatment

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:02.965
in horror movies, like footsteps.

00:04:02.965 --> 00:04:06.760
Alchemy has a huge shoe
collection for this purpose.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:08.960
I know how each one of these shoes reacts

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.630
to every one of these floor surfaces.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:13.437
Narrator: In a non-horror
movie, you'll likely know

00:04:13.437 --> 00:04:15.732
exactly where footstep
sounds are coming from

00:04:15.732 --> 00:04:18.290
and how close they are
to the central character.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:19.892
But in horror movies,

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:23.540
sometimes the most effective
footsteps are barely audible,

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.060
so you really have to strain to hear them,

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.320
just as the protagonist is doing.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.940
To soften the footsteps
of the Invisible Man

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.260
and match the sound of his neoprene suit,

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:36.690
Leslie walked around with
rubber gloves over his feet,

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.733
adding a yoga mat to mute
the sounds even more.

00:04:40.835 --> 00:04:43.668
[quiet footsteps]

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:45.680
The result?

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.343
You might not even be sure
you heard anything at all.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:52.160
That uncertainty is the
bread and butter of suspense

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:53.620
in horror movies.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.858
Barely perceptible sounds can make you

00:04:55.858 --> 00:04:57.750
doubt your own senses,

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:00.100
just like the character
is doubting theirs.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:03.880
And that makes the viewing
experience that much scarier.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.110
What places you even further
into the protagonist's shoes,

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:10.380
literally, is the sound
of their footsteps.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.480
She is, like, nervously shaking.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:14.795
I'm going to try to portray that

00:05:14.795 --> 00:05:17.090
in her movements, in her footsteps.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:19.757
[wood creaking]

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.430
Narrator: To play this
up, the artists at Alchemy

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:27.060
don't use just any regular floor.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.500
I need old, crappy wood,

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:32.100
I need a really bad subfloor,

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:33.903
and I need really crappy nails

00:05:33.903 --> 00:05:35.770
that are going to move within the wood.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:37.210
Narrator: Leslie made a custom one

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:39.240
that met all those requirements,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:41.930
partly using 65-year-old wood.

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:45.120
Each corner makes slightly
different kinds of creaky sounds,

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.420
which emphasize the sense of unsteadiness.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.963
Like you're -- again,
literally -- on shaky ground.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.501
To cap it all off, there's this.

00:05:56.501 --> 00:05:57.334
[handle squeaks]

00:05:57.334 --> 00:05:58.990
The accent sound.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:00.678
That's a sharp, pronounced sound

00:06:00.678 --> 00:06:02.461
that stands out against the subtler,

00:06:02.461 --> 00:06:04.577
more ambient ones we've been hearing,

00:06:04.577 --> 00:06:08.620
indicating to the audience
that something is off.

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:12.302
Leslie used an old sink to
create this unsettling squeak.

00:06:12.302 --> 00:06:13.600
[handle squeaks]

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.330
But that came out sounding
like too big a piece of metal,

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:19.365
so he used a towel to dampen the sound.

00:06:19.365 --> 00:06:20.550
[handle squeaks]

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.531
But what really makes this

00:06:22.531 --> 00:06:23.364
[handle squeaks]

00:06:23.364 --> 00:06:24.630
[silence]

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:25.987
scarier than this

00:06:25.987 --> 00:06:27.628
[handle squeaks]

00:06:27.628 --> 00:06:29.370
[water drains]

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.450
is the way everything else goes quiet

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:32.943
after the sink squeaks.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:36.541
It's not realistic for all the sounds

00:06:36.541 --> 00:06:39.174
to suddenly stop right
when the faucet does,

00:06:39.174 --> 00:06:42.543
but sound in horror movies
isn't always about realism.

00:06:42.543 --> 00:06:45.334
Often it's about creating
a heightened reality.

00:06:45.334 --> 00:06:47.537
It doesn't have to be
wall-to-wall sound all the time.

00:06:47.537 --> 00:06:51.069
Having that brief moment of just silence

00:06:51.069 --> 00:06:53.002
and then, "Wham!"

00:06:53.002 --> 00:06:54.160
To me, that's powerful.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:56.620
Narrator: Here, the few
moments of absolute quiet

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:57.895
put the audience on edge

00:06:57.895 --> 00:07:00.220
right before the moment of attack.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:02.120
And when that moment does come,

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.113
the contrast is that much stronger.

00:07:05.342 --> 00:07:06.842
[thud]

00:07:06.842 --> 00:07:08.525
[Cecilia choking]

00:07:08.525 --> 00:07:10.230
Leslie: I'd like to make this grab

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:12.433
just a little bit larger than life.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:17.320
[thud]

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.200
Let's get a grab on more skin.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:20.963
We'll layer the two of those together.

00:07:22.121 --> 00:07:22.954
[smack]

00:07:22.954 --> 00:07:25.320
And I want to throw in,
like, a little bit of creak,

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.560
just when she's getting lifted up.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:31.290
[smack]
[rubber squeaking]

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:33.890
That's a hit and a grab
and the whole nine yards,

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:36.260
so, you know, she's in trouble.

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:37.820
Narrator: Now let's watch another scene

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:40.820
and see how these suspense
sounds play out together.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.110
The accent sound comes
earlier in this scene

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:45.739
in the form of a jarring phone ring.

00:07:45.739 --> 00:07:47.810
[phone buzzes]

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:50.320
The phone has an unnaturally loud echo

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:51.310
against the wood.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.690
[phone buzzes]

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.870
And it's startling because of its contrast

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:57.870
with the otherwise quiet attic.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.620
But that's sort of a fake-out,

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:01.940
because afterwards we return to quiet,

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:05.370
which can give the audience
a sense of false calm.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:07.700
Cecilia's shaky, creaky footsteps

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.070
are actually crawling
sounds in this scene,

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:11.950
which put the viewer right there with her

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:14.481
as she moves across the attic.

00:08:14.481 --> 00:08:17.790
[gentle thudding]

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:20.910
Leslie accentuated the sound
of the creaking floorboards

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:23.873
using his custom-made "creak box."

00:08:23.873 --> 00:08:26.540
[wood creaking]

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:32.870
Earlier we saw how subtle
footsteps and whooshes

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.710
signaled the bad guy's presence.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:38.260
Here, it's the faint rattle
of a ladder in the distance.

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:41.485
She hears something,

00:08:41.485 --> 00:08:43.265
and it jars her.

00:08:43.265 --> 00:08:45.000
[faint rattle]

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.760
Now, we don't need climbing up the ladder,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:48.780
because that would be just --

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:50.346
we've given away the farm.

00:08:50.346 --> 00:08:51.971
Not too loud, not too big.

00:08:51.971 --> 00:08:54.326
He's trying to sneak up on her, too.

00:08:54.326 --> 00:08:55.159
[ladder rattles]

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.740
Narrator: Since all we
hear is that rattle,

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.140
we don't know exactly
where the bad guy is,

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:02.400
throwing our senses off yet again.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:06.263
That's crucial because it's
priming us for this jump scare.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:11.263
Part of why that reveal is so terrifying

00:09:11.263 --> 00:09:14.020
is because of the proximity
of the Invisible Man.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:16.590
By giving us minimal hints
as to his whereabouts,

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:18.535
the sound design tricked us into thinking

00:09:18.535 --> 00:09:19.800
he was farther away.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:21.435
Leslie: When he gets splashed with paint,

00:09:21.435 --> 00:09:23.685
[rattling]

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.660
at that point, the cat's out of the bag.

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:27.830
Narrator: The big moment here called

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:29.839
for some bigger and messier sounds.

00:09:29.839 --> 00:09:30.910
[sheepskin splats]

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:32.549
Leslie: We don't want to
dump paint everywhere,

00:09:32.549 --> 00:09:34.590
but we need it to sound thick and gooey.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:36.080
Narrator: The best tool for that

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:37.840
are these scraps of sheepskin,

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:39.881
which Leslie says are "nastier"

00:09:39.881 --> 00:09:42.100
than your usual towel or rag.

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:44.600
Leslie: But it holds a ton of water in it,

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:46.650
which gives it a very, you know,

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:51.335
very gooey, visceral kind of sound to it.

00:09:51.335 --> 00:09:53.140
[splashing]

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:55.360
Narrator: To maximize moments like this,

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:57.670
horror movies have to walk a fine line,

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.793
figuring out how to hide a character

00:09:59.793 --> 00:10:02.140
while revealing them at the same time.

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:05.110
They accomplish this with
subtle Foley and sound design

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.610
that goes beyond realism to
create a heightened reality

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:16.437
where your ears and mind
are playing tricks on you.

00:10:16.437 --> 00:10:19.505
[footsteps]

00:10:19.505 --> 00:10:21.538
[creaking]

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:24.388
[TV clicks off]

00:10:24.388 --> 00:10:26.816
Man: Don't let him haunt you.

00:10:26.816 --> 00:10:27.649
Cecilia: Hello?

00:10:28.556 --> 00:10:33.556
[heavy breathing]

